  "primary_meaning": "Gentile believers are incorporated into the covenant people of God through faith, sharing in the spiritual blessings and privileges of the olive tree (Israel) while natural branches (unbelieving Jews) are broken off due to unbelief.

content.text:
{
  "primary_meaning": "Gentile believers being incorporated (as wild olive branches) into the cultivated olive tree representing the covenant people of God / Israel, thereby sharing in the root and rich sap of the Abrahamic promises and blessings while some natural branches are broken off for unbelief (with the possibility of re-grafting).",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Romans 11:17-24",
    "Romans 11:11-36"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "Supersessionist reading in which the olive tree is identified primarily with the Church that replaces ethnic Israel, so that Gentiles constitute the new permanent people of God.",
      "basis": [
        "Romans 11:17-24 (read as permanent displacement of the natural branches)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Emphasis on the temporary nature of Jewish unbelief and the future mass re-grafting of ethnic Israel into their own olive tree, with Gentiles as co-participants rather than replacements.",
      "basis": [
        "Romans 11:23-26",
        "Romans 11:11-15"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Personal/spiritual application to individual believers’ vital union with Christ or the source of spiritual life, sometimes conflated with the vine-and-branches imagery.",
      "basis": [
        "Romans 11:17-24",
        "John 15:1-8 (by analogy)"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "The grafting metaphor is almost exclusively derived from Paul’s explicit olive-tree allegory in Romans 11 and is one of the most consistently recognized New Testament symbols for the relationship between Israel and Gentile believers / the Church. Variations concern ecclesiological implications (inclusion vs. replacement, present vs. future status of Israel) rather than the core agricultural image itself."
}
